/**
@defgroup ref results that hold the bytes they point into

`cbor::ref<T>` holds a value and the owner of the bytes that its views
point into. `cbor::owning<T>` holds a copy of a value and of the bytes
of its views. Include `<cbor/cbor.hpp>`.

A text or a byte string comes back as a view into the bytes, and no byte
is copied. The view sits in a `cbor::ref<T>`. The `cbor::ref<T>` holds
the owner, so the view stays valid while the `cbor::ref<T>` lives.

A copy of a `cbor::ref<T>` shares the owner and copies no bytes.

@section ref_owner THE OWNER OF THE BYTES

The owner is a `std::shared_ptr<void const>`. Bytes come into the library
in one of these forms. No form copies the bytes.

- An rvalue `std::string` is moved into a new owner. `cbor::lazy::adopt`
  makes it.
- A `std::shared_ptr<std::string const>` is read in place. `cbor::lazy::view`
  takes it from the caller, and every result keeps it alive.
- A `std::shared_ptr<void const>` and a `std::string_view` beside it are
  read in place. The owner keeps the bytes of the view alive. The library
  does not check that the owner holds them.

A `std::string` lvalue beside an owner does not compile. Its bytes can end
before the owner ends.

`cbor::structs<T>::%decode`, `cbor::schema<T>::%decode`, `cbor::schema<T>::%path`
and `cbor::at_path` take the same forms for a view result. `cbor::sequence`
takes the two forms that use a `std::shared_ptr`, and no rvalue
`std::string`. The library takes no other pointer to bytes.

A larger object can hold the bytes. Two forms read them in place. The
aliasing constructor of `std::shared_ptr` makes an owner from a member
of the object, and that owner shares the count of the object. Or the
object is the owner, and a view of its bytes is passed beside it.

A `cbor::lazy` holds the owner of its bytes. So does each `cbor::lazy`
that a step gives, and each iterator of its views. A `cbor::lazy` from a
step stays valid after the `cbor::lazy` that gave it ends.

@section ref_read READ THE VALUE

`*r` gives the value of a `cbor::ref<T>` `r`. A view comes back by value.
Any other `T` comes back as a `T const &`. `r->` reaches the members of
the value.

For a `cbor::ref<std::string_view>`, `*r` is the `std::string_view`. The
view stays valid while `r` lives. A view copied out of `r` does not keep
the bytes. Keep the `cbor::ref<T>` in a variable as long as its views are
in use.

`*` and `->` on a temporary `cbor::ref<T>` do not compile. `==` works on
a temporary, because it gives a `bool`. `==` compares the value with a
`T`, and it compares two `cbor::ref<T>` by their values.

@section ref_copy COPY THE VALUE

`clone()` copies the bytes into new memory. The copy stays valid after
the owner ends.

- For a `cbor::ref<std::string_view>`, `clone()` gives a `std::string`.
- For a `cbor::ref<std::span<std::byte const>>`, `clone()` gives a
  `std::vector<std::byte>`.
- For any other `T`, `clone()` gives a `cbor::owning<T>`.

@section ref_share SHARE AND MOVE

A copy of a `cbor::ref<T>` shares the owner and copies the value. A move
takes the owner and the value. The moved-from `cbor::ref<T>` holds `T{}`
and no owner.

Only the library makes a `cbor::ref<T>`. A program gets one from a call.

@section ref_owning THE OWNING VALUE

`clone()` of a `cbor::ref<T>` gives a `cbor::owning<T>` for a `T` that is
not a single text or byte view. The `cbor::owning<T>` holds a copy of the
value and of the bytes of each view in it. A view in it stays valid while
the `cbor::owning<T>` or one of its copies lives.

`*o` gives the value as a `T const &`, and `o->` reaches its members. The
value is `const`. `*` and `->` on a temporary `cbor::owning<T>` do not
compile.

A copy of a `cbor::owning<T>` shares the value and copies no bytes. A
`cbor::owning<T>` has no `==`, and no public constructor.

@section ref_errors ERRORS

A `cbor::ref<T>` and a `cbor::owning<T>` give no error code. A wrong use
does not compile: `*` and `->` on a temporary, and a `std::string` lvalue
beside an owner. A null owner, or an empty owner in the form with a view,
given to `cbor::lazy::view` throws `std::logic_error`.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_ref.cpp ref group
*/
